<Group labelreference="L273" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">
We're going to build a classification problem in three dimensions using a degree 2 polynomial feature map. 

We'll start by initializing a 3-d manifold in which the original datapoints are given.
</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" hide-output="false" labelreference="L227" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">DGEnvironment[Manifold]([x,y,z],M)</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="LUkjbWlHNiMvSSttb2R1bGVuYW1lRzYiSSxUeXBlc2V0dGluZ0dJKF9zeXNsaWJHRic2JVEsTWFuaWZvbGQ6fk1GJy8lJ2l0YWxpY0dRJXRydWVGJy8lLG1hdGh2YXJpYW50R1EnaXRhbGljRic=">SSxNYW5pZm9sZDp+TUc2Ig==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L276" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">
Here we'll apply logistic regression, which assigns a probability&gt;0.5 when z&gt;0 and probability&lt;0.5 when z&lt;0:
</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" hide-output="false" labelreference="L275" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field prompt="M &gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">sigmoid:=1/(1+exp(-z)):
plot(sigmoid);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="Maple Plot" layout="Maple Plot"><Plot height="400.0" type="two-dimensional" width="400.0" plot-scale="1.0" plot-xtrans="0.0" plot-ytrans="0.0" gridlinevisibility="1" legendvisibility="false"></Plot></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L282" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">
Now the trick is that &quot;z&quot; is going to be x^2+y^2/2 -yz + z^2/2: if this is positive at a point then the point belongs to class 1 and if this is negative then the point belongs to class 0.
</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" hide-output="false" labelreference="L274" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field prompt="M &gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">zee:=x^2+y^2/2-y*z+z^2/2;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LCoqJEkieEclKnByb3RlY3RlZEciIiMiIiIqJEkieUdGJUYmI0YnRiYqJkYpRidJInpHRiVGJyEiIiokRixGJkYq</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L272" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">
We're going to build a classification problem 
</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" hide-output="false" labelreference="L270" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field prompt="M &gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">f:=eval(sigmoid,z=zee);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">KiQsJiIiIkYkLUkkZXhwRzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMsKiokSSJ4R0YoIiIjISIiKiRJInlHRihGLyNGMEYvKiZGMkYkSSJ6R0YoRiRGJCokRjVGL0YzRiRGMA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L268" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">
One way to do this is to move to a feature space in which &quot;z&quot; is (hopefully) a hyperplane.

Our feature map into 6 dimensions will be x1=x^2, x2=sqrt(2)*x*y, x3=sqrt(2)*x*z, x4=y^2, x5=sqrt(2)*y*z, x6=z^2.
</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L267" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">
If you get to reading about kernels and feature maps in machine learning, here is the kernel for this feature map:
</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" hide-output="false" labelreference="L265" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field prompt="M &gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">kernel:=(x1*z1+x2*z2+x3*z3)^2</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">KiQsKComSSN4MUc2IiIiIkkjejFHRiZGJ0YnKiZJI3gyR0YmRidJI3oyR0YmRidGJyomSSN4M0dGJkYnSSN6M0dGJkYnRiciIiM=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" hide-output="false" labelreference="L264" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field prompt="M &gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">expand(kernel)</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LC4qJkkjeDFHNiIiIiNJI3oxR0YlRiYiIiIqKkYkRihJI3gyR0YlRihGJ0YoSSN6MkdGJUYoRiYqKkYkRihJI3gzR0YlRihGJ0YoSSN6M0dGJUYoRiYqJkYqRiZGK0YmRigqKkYqRihGLUYoRitGKEYuRihGJiomRi1GJkYuRiZGKA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
